# Time as an interval

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:ex_tempo, "~> 1.4"},
  {:ical, "~> 3.2"},
  {:tz, "~> 0.28"}
])

Application.put_env(:elixir, :time_zone_database, Tz.TimeZoneDatabase)
```

## Tempo

<!-- slide: logo https://raw.githubusercontent.com/kipcole9/tempo/refs/heads/main/assets/logo.png -->

<!-- slide: author Kip Cole -->

<!-- slide: byline Author of Tempo, Localize, Elixir Unicode, Image, Text, ... -->

<!-- slide: event Melbourne Elixir Meetup -->

<!-- slide: date 3 September 2026 -->

One idea, followed all the way down. Every example tonight builds one thing: the calendar for this meetup — when it runs, when it clashes with a public holiday, and when a speaker is actually free to rehearse.

* Every date and time is a **bounded interval**, never an instant
* One type — `%Tempo{}` — carries its own resolution
* Set algebra falls out: union, intersection, difference, overlap
* Tonight's thread: **the Melbourne Elixir Meetup calendar**

## Where it fits

Elixir's `Date`, `Time`, `DateTime` and `NaiveDateTime` are excellent scalars. Tempo is not a replacement for them — it is the layer above, for the questions scalars make awkward.

| Question | Scalars | Tempo |
| --- | --- | --- |
| "Is this the day, or midnight on it?" | ambiguous | it *is* the day |
| "Last Friday of the month" | helper function | `-1FR` |
| "Sometime in the 1560s" | inexpressible | `~o"156X"` |
| "When are we both free?" | hand-rolled | set difference |
| "Every second Tuesday, forever" | a library | a value |

## Three classes of bug it removes

Prose here is the argument I want to make out loud, not text to read off the wall.

* **Ambiguity** — is `~D[2026-06-15]` midnight, or the whole day? Everyone writes `end_of_day/1`
* **Type explosion** — `Date`, `Time`, `DateTime`, and lossy conversions between them
* **The expressiveness ceiling** — recurrences, uncertainty, open-ended spans
* Once every value is an interval, comparison and set algebra are *free*

## A date is an interval

The value is not a point that we later stretch into a range. Its bounds are real and Tempo will show them to you.

<!-- slide: code -->

```elixir
import Tempo.Sigils

case Tempo.to_interval(~o"2026-09-02") do
  {:ok, interval} ->
    Tempo.Interval.endpoints(interval)

  {:error, reason} ->
    reason
end
```

## One type, any resolution

A year, a month, a meeting, a millennium — the same struct. The resolution is carried by the value, not by its type.

Half-open `[from, to)` throughout, so adjacent intervals join with no overlap and no gap.

<!-- slide: code -->

```elixir
for value <- [~o"2026", ~o"2026-09", ~o"2026-09-02", ~o"2026-09-02T18:00"] do
  {:ok, interval} = Tempo.to_interval(value)
  {value, Tempo.Interval.endpoints(interval)}
end
```

## ISO 8601 is more expressive than we use

Most of us know a tenth of the standard. Tempo implements Parts 1 *and* 2, which is where the interesting shapes live.

* `2026-09-02` — a day
* `2026-W36` — an ISO week
* `2026-245` — an ordinal date
* `156X` — the 1560s, a decade with an unknown digit
* `2026-09-02T18:00[Australia/Melbourne]` — IXDTF, zone in the value

## Counting from the end

ISO 8601-2 §4.4.1 lets any component be negative, meaning *count back from the end of the containing unit*. No `days_in_month/2`, no leap-year branch.

<!-- slide: code -->

```elixir
last_day = fn month ->
  {:ok, set} = Tempo.select(month, ~o"-1D")

  set
  |> Tempo.IntervalSet.members()
  |> hd()
  |> Tempo.Interval.from()
  |> Tempo.day()
end

{last_day.(~o"2024-02"), last_day.(~o"2026-02")}
```

## Partial dates are real values

A birthday is not a date. It is a day-and-month that recurs, and most systems store it as a date in an arbitrary year and then apologise.

`XX` says *"this component is unknown"* — so `1985-XX-15` is the 15th of every month, and `XXXX-09-02` is the 2nd of September in any year.

<!-- slide: code -->

```elixir
{:ok, fifteenths} = Tempo.to_interval(~o"1985-XX-15")

{Tempo.IntervalSet.count(fifteenths),
 fifteenths |> Tempo.IntervalSet.members() |> Enum.take(3) |> Enum.map(&Tempo.to_iso8601/1)}
```

## Enumeration, implicitly

Every Tempo coarser than its finest unit is enumerable, and it walks its own sub-units. Nothing to configure — the value already knows what it contains.

<!-- slide: code -->

```elixir
%{
  "a year"  => ~o"2026" |> Enum.take(3) |> Enum.map(&Tempo.to_iso8601/1),
  "a month" => ~o"2026-09" |> Enum.take(3) |> Enum.map(&Tempo.to_iso8601/1),
  "a day"   => ~o"2026-09-02" |> Enum.take(3) |> Enum.map(&Tempo.to_iso8601/1),
  "a decade with an unknown digit" => ~o"202X" |> Enum.take(4) |> Enum.map(&Tempo.to_iso8601/1)
}
```

## Enumeration, explicitly

Any component may carry a range, a step, or a set — so the *shape* of the recurrence is part of the literal rather than an argument to a function.

<!-- slide: code -->

```elixir
%{
  "months 1..3"        => ~o"2026Y{1..3}M" |> Enum.take(3) |> Enum.map(&Tempo.to_iso8601/1),
  "every second week"  => ~o"2026Y{1..10//2}W" |> Enum.take(5) |> Enum.map(&Tempo.to_iso8601/1),
  "just these years"   => ~o"[1984,1986,1988]" |> Enum.take(3) |> Enum.map(&Tempo.to_iso8601/1)
}
```

## Our meetup, as a value

First Wednesday of the month, 18:00 to 20:00, Melbourne time. That is an RFC 5545 rule, and Tempo parses `RRULE` into the same interval machinery as everything else.

<!-- slide: code -->

```elixir
meetups =
  with {:ok, rule} <-
         Tempo.RRule.parse("FREQ=MONTHLY;BYDAY=1WE;COUNT=6",
           from: ~o"2026-09-02T18:00:00[Australia/Melbourne]"
         ),
       {:ok, set} <- Tempo.to_interval(rule) do
    set
  end

meetups
|> Tempo.IntervalSet.members()
|> Enum.map(&Tempo.to_iso8601(Tempo.Interval.from(&1)))
```

## Set operations

Four operations and a handful of predicates, over any combination of Tempo values — across resolutions, zones and calendars.

* `union/2` — everything in either
* `intersection/2` — everything in both
* `difference/2` — in the first, not the second
* `overlaps?/2`, `subset?/2`, `contains?/2`

## Holidays arrive as an .ics

Nobody types their public holidays in by hand. They come from a calendar feed, and an `.ics` is a set of intervals wearing a costume.

<!-- slide: code -->

```elixir
holidays_ics = """
BEGIN:VCALENDAR
VERSION:2.0
PRODID:-//Melbourne//Public Holidays//EN
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:cup-2026
SUMMARY:Melbourne Cup Day
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261103
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261104
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:christmas-2026
SUMMARY:Christmas Day
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261225
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261226
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:venue-closed-2026
SUMMARY:Venue closed for works
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261130
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261205
END:VEVENT
END:VCALENDAR
"""

{:ok, holidays} = Tempo.ICal.from_ical(holidays_ics)

# The set inspects with each interval's summary intact — the point of the slide.
holidays
```

## Composing them

The metadata survives the operation. An interval that came from a `VEVENT` still knows its summary after being intersected with something else — which is why the answer can explain itself.

<!-- slide: code -->

```elixir
# The first operand's metadata survives, so ask the holidays which
# meetup they hit rather than the other way round.
with {:ok, clashes} <- Tempo.intersection(holidays, meetups),
     {:ok, survivors} <- Tempo.difference(meetups, holidays) do
  %{
    clashes:
      Enum.map(
        Tempo.IntervalSet.members(clashes),
        &{Tempo.to_iso8601(Tempo.Interval.from(&1)), Tempo.Interval.metadata(&1)[:summary]}
      ),
    meetups_left: Tempo.IntervalSet.count(survivors)
  }
end
```

## Free and busy is set difference

This is the whole trick. "Free" is not a stored state to be kept in sync — it is *open minus busy*, computed on every call, so it cannot go stale and no release step can be forgotten.

<!-- slide: code -->

```elixir
rehearsal_window = ~o"2026-09-01T09:00:00[Australia/Melbourne]/2026-09-01T17:00:00[Australia/Melbourne]"

commitments =
  Tempo.IntervalSet.new!([
    ~o"2026-09-01T10:00:00[Australia/Melbourne]/2026-09-01T11:30:00[Australia/Melbourne]",
    ~o"2026-09-01T13:00:00[Australia/Melbourne]/2026-09-01T14:00:00[Australia/Melbourne]"
  ])

# `free` inspects to legible `~o` intervals — no need to map it to strings.
{:ok, free} = Tempo.difference(rehearsal_window, commitments)
free
```

## Daylight saving, unasked

Melbourne moves to AEDT on the first Sunday in October. A meetup that is "18:00 to 20:00 local" is two hours long in September and two hours long in October — but the *instants* differ by an hour.

Wall clock and elapsed time are different questions, and the zone belongs in the value rather than in an argument you might forget to pass.

<!-- slide: code -->

```elixir
# No literal dates: reuse the meetup calendar, and let `at/2` set the
# 20:00 close. The same wall-clock window (18:00–20:00 Melbourne)
# projects to a different UTC instant once the city moves to AEDT.
meetups
|> Tempo.IntervalSet.members()
|> Enum.take(3)
|> Enum.map(fn meetup ->
  opening = Tempo.Interval.from(meetup)
  closing = Tempo.at!(opening, ~o"T20")

  with {:ok, opening_utc} <- Tempo.shift_zone(opening, "Etc/UTC"),
       {:ok, closing_utc} <- Tempo.shift_zone(closing, "Etc/UTC") do
    %{
      melbourne: Tempo.to_iso8601(opening),
      utc: Tempo.to_iso8601(opening_utc) <> " → " <> Tempo.to_iso8601(closing_utc)
    }
  end
end)
```

## The DST trap, avoided

The spring-forward night is where hand-rolled arithmetic quietly breaks: 02:00 does not exist, so "add 24 hours" and "same time tomorrow" stop being the same question.

* A UTC-only database resolves every zone to UTC and is **silently wrong**, not loud
* Configure a real IANA database — `Tz`, `Tzdata`, `TimeZoneInfo`, `Zoneinfo`
* Tempo takes the zone from the value, so it cannot be forgotten at the call site
* Set operations compare by UTC and return the first operand's zone

## When the dates are not known

Sometimes you do not have a calendar, you have constraints — "K1 reigned before K2", "the stratum lasted twenty to a hundred years". A chronological network propagates those into the tightest bounds the evidence supports.

<!-- slide: code -->

```elixir
alias Tempo.Network

network =
  Network.new()
  |> Network.add_period(:cfp, name: "Call for papers", start: {:not_before, ~o"2026-06-01"}, duration: {:at_most, ~o"P8W"})
  |> Network.add_period(:review, name: "Review", duration: {:at_least, ~o"P2W"})
  |> Network.add_period(:conference, name: "Conference", end: {:not_after, ~o"2026-12-01"})
  |> Network.add_relation(:before, :cfp, :review)
  |> Network.add_relation(:before, :review, :conference)

{:ok, solved} = Network.Solver.tighten(network)
review = solved.periods[:review]
%{earliest_start: review.earliest_start, latest_end: review.latest_end}
```

## The trace

No hidden assumptions. Every computed bound can name the inputs it rests on, in the order they apply — so a colleague who disputes the conclusion can point at exactly which input they reject.

<!-- slide: code -->

```elixir
{:ok, trace} = Network.Solver.trace(network, {:start, :conference}, bound: :earliest)
trace.prose
```

## Why a network, not a schedule

A schedule says *when things are*. A network says *what you know*, and works out what follows.

* Bounds propagate both ways — a late conference pulls the CFP earlier
* Every bound carries a **trace**: the chain of constraints that forces it
* Contradictions are reported, not silently resolved
* Built for archaeology; useful anywhere the dates are inferred

## What Tempo is not

Being honest about the edges is more useful than a feature list.

* Not a replacement for `Date`/`DateTime` — it is the layer above them
* Not a scheduler — that is **Agenda**, which is built on it
* Not fast at instant arithmetic; it is *expressive* at interval arithmetic
* Needs a real timezone database; it will not pick one for you

